WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.640
The Amazon rainforest
is one of the largest,

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:11.670
if not the largest in the world.

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:13.110
Rainforests are critically important

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:16.120
for the global climate in many ways.

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:18.570
It regulates temperature
and weather cycles

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:21.880
in the Americas, and it
actually carries rain

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:23.280
to much of the continent,

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:25.523
which is a bread basket for the world.

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:29.370
They have what are called
flying rivers above the jungle.

00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:32.520
These bands of moisture that really affect

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:35.590
and regulate weather patterns

00:00:35.590 --> 00:00:37.120
far from the Amazon,

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:40.230
and even more critical
now with climate change,

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:41.800
the destruction of the Amazon,

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:43.740
which has been accumulating over decades

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:47.540
is accelerating almost at
the point of no return.

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:50.050
Scientists are warning
that we're getting closer

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:51.630
and closer to a tipping point,

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:53.610
that we may even be there already.

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:55.450
So if we pass that tipping point,

00:00:55.450 --> 00:00:59.920
we start seeing the rainforest
turning into a savanna,

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:02.850
where you don't have the
trees that are able to

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:05.840
pull all that pollution out of the air.

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:09.310
You don't have the ecosystem
that's producing the rainfall

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:10.853
that feeds the continent.

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:16.760
Brazil's government says it's
working to stop deforestation,

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:18.500
but behind the scenes,

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:21.640
it's actively engaged in a campaign

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:24.253
to prioritize and develop the Amazon.

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:28.540
And it's really being
fueled and encouraged

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:31.810
by a government that really
believes that the Amazon

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:34.750
is there to be developed,
to be privatized,

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:37.863
to be exploited for its natural riches.

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:41.860
Unless we find a way to reduce

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:43.810
the sort of voracious demand in the world

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:46.490
for the commodities that
come out of the Amazon,

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:48.640
it's really hard to see how

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:52.540
this cycle of destruction
will really ever end.

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:54.490
And eventually, it's gonna be too late.

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:07.070
If you want to know why the
Amazon is being destroyed,

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:09.480
it's not because people
just want to destroy it.

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:11.610
It's because they're doing it

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:14.110
for the things that we're
consuming far, far away.

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:17.650
You have to understand
it from the perspective

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:19.590
of certain forces in Brazil,

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:23.323
especially the forces
behind ranching and logging.

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:28.380
You really just need to go
to your local Home Depot

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:31.710
and look at the hardwoods
that are being sold there,

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:32.557
many of which come from Brazil

00:02:32.557 --> 00:02:34.410
and many of which come from the Amazon,

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:37.400
and those are going on
your living room floor.

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:40.070
Or think about it the next
time you have a barbecue

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:44.350
and you're enjoying a really
nice steak or you buy a burger.

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:46.000
Well, the beef that you're eating

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:48.460
is part of this global beef system

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:49.933
that starts in the Amazon.

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:55.389
As much as the messaging
is that they don't want

00:02:55.389 --> 00:02:58.400
people in the Amazon to
be burning the Amazon,

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:00.350
what the world is really
telling these people

00:03:00.350 --> 00:03:03.250
is that we will pay
you to burn the Amazon,

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:06.573
because we will buy everything
that you're selling us.

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:11.070
The Amazon rainforest, it's
lost in the past 40 years,

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:12.810
an area the size of California.

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:14.830
There was progress that was being made

00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:16.680
in slowing those burns.

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:18.240
That's changed.

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:20.130
The burns are accelerating this year.

00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:22.393
They were at an 11 year high last year.

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:26.370
So why isn't anyone
being held accountable?

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:29.050
This was a story that
was a year in the making.

00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:31.350
And the more I started digging,

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:34.430
it became obvious that
everyone in the Amazon region

00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:36.040
is seeking a piece of land

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:39.300
and grabbing a piece of public land.

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:44.060
And the government is not
only looking the other way,

00:03:44.060 --> 00:03:46.560
but in many ways, they're
actually encouraging it.

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:51.750
After spending some time on the ground

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:55.970
and talking to dozens,
dozens and dozens of ranchers

00:03:55.970 --> 00:03:58.650
of enforcers, of park rangers,

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:01.780
of people within Bolsonaro's inner circle,

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:05.140
it becomes clear that everything pushes

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:06.820
these ranchers and these farmers

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:09.110
towards the decisions that they make.

00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:12.260
Not only do we have the
insatiable demand for commodities,

00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:14.480
but it's rooted in Brazil's culture,

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:16.423
and it's rooted in Brazil's history.

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:26.000
In the 70s and 80s, the Brazilian policy

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:29.100
was very much to encourage
industrialized farmers

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:31.440
and manufacturers into the Amazon,

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:33.690
because they wanted to develop the Amazon

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:37.263
as Brazil's ticket to first world status.

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:41.150
This was during the Brazilian miracle,

00:04:41.150 --> 00:04:43.900
years of extremely high economic growth

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:47.790
when the government was
calling people to the Amazon.

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:50.520
A lot of poor Brazilians
had been working this land

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:53.980
for decades, and yet they
had no claim to this land,

00:04:53.980 --> 00:04:57.810
and so the policy shifted from encouraging

00:04:57.810 --> 00:05:01.040
the big farmers and favoring the big guy

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:04.160
into let's help the little guy.

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:06.860
And it was one of the biggest
social welfare giveaways

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:09.970
of all time, where they
started handing out

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:13.600
government land in the
Amazon to small farmers,

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:15.470
to poor Brazilians.

00:05:15.470 --> 00:05:17.980
You had TV ads, you had radio ads,

00:05:17.980 --> 00:05:22.040
you had ads in magazines that
drove people to the Amazon

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:23.860
and encouraged millions of people

00:05:23.860 --> 00:05:27.250
to move from the coastal
cities into the Amazon

00:05:27.250 --> 00:05:29.750
and conquer what they were
calling the green hell.

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:32.023
And they said it was

00:05:32.023 --> 00:05:34.830
a land without men for men without land.

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:39.260
That's what drove this
massive migration of people

00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:40.363
to the Amazon,

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:43.380
to stake their claim in the Amazon,

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:45.570
to sort of be homesteaders,

00:05:45.570 --> 00:05:48.620
to clear a piece of land

00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:50.690
and make something of it and contribute

00:05:50.690 --> 00:05:53.403
to this rapaciously
growing economy of Brazil.

00:05:56.410 --> 00:06:01.410
That message several decades
later still persists.

00:06:02.050 --> 00:06:04.590
It's very much in the Brazilian culture

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:08.293
that in order to prosper,
you need to have land.

00:06:10.750 --> 00:06:14.130
There's almost a roadmap to
how deforestation happens.

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:16.610
It's a very predictable cycle.

00:06:16.610 --> 00:06:20.320
In the Amazon, you have a mess
of a land management system,

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:22.410
and so there's a lot of public land

00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:25.853
that hasn't been designated
for a specific purpose.

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:29.590
Typically poor Brazilians will go in,

00:06:29.590 --> 00:06:32.920
they're the foot soldiers of
deforestation in a lot of ways.

00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:36.100
They'll claim this land,
they'll band together

00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:38.553
and really create these societies.

00:06:40.220 --> 00:06:42.630
The hardwoods are always the first to go.

00:06:42.630 --> 00:06:46.060
And so the easiest way to sort of survive

00:06:46.060 --> 00:06:49.370
is to get cattle and sell it to

00:06:49.370 --> 00:06:52.023
the internal market or the global market.

00:06:54.210 --> 00:06:56.180
They don't last too long on the land,

00:06:56.180 --> 00:06:59.670
'cause it's very difficult
to make a living in the end.

00:06:59.670 --> 00:07:01.550
And so a lot of these families

00:07:01.550 --> 00:07:02.990
are pushed off their land eventually

00:07:02.990 --> 00:07:04.390
or bought out of their land.

00:07:05.570 --> 00:07:08.800
And it's at that point
that the big ranchers

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:11.630
and the big farmers come in

00:07:11.630 --> 00:07:13.450
and they start gathering this land

00:07:13.450 --> 00:07:15.890
and accumulating land empires,

00:07:15.890 --> 00:07:19.240
and they're able to say
we didn't clear the land.

00:07:19.240 --> 00:07:21.190
We weren't the ones who burned it down.

00:07:22.470 --> 00:07:25.040
They build their empires
and they start producing

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:29.020
soy beans, coffee, cacao.

00:07:29.020 --> 00:07:32.660
In Brazil, these are not
small farmer products.

00:07:32.660 --> 00:07:35.200
They require irrigation and investment,

00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:37.070
and they require a lot of money.

00:07:37.070 --> 00:07:41.353
It's typically the big
farmers who get into this.

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:48.060
The Bolsonaro administration
didn't invent the practice

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:50.880
of turning a blind eye
as the Amazon is raided

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:54.080
and granting amnesty afterwards.

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:57.360
However, the government
through its actions

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:00.463
is accelerating those land
grabs like never before.

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:05.825
They have stripped the regulating agencies

00:08:05.825 --> 00:08:07.700
of their manpower,

00:08:07.700 --> 00:08:09.420
the budgets have been cut.

00:08:09.420 --> 00:08:12.450
The people in charge of
land management agencies

00:08:12.450 --> 00:08:16.740
are ranchers and farmers
who have a direct stake

00:08:16.740 --> 00:08:19.250
in making sure that the
system keeps running.

00:08:19.250 --> 00:08:23.290
And possibly most damaging
is that the government

00:08:23.290 --> 00:08:28.290
wants to amnesty more recent invasions

00:08:28.370 --> 00:08:31.223
and bigger invasions than ever before.

00:08:35.480 --> 00:08:39.970
Scientists tell us that
this is the decisive decade.

00:08:39.970 --> 00:08:42.480
President Biden of the
United States, again,

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:44.270
to try to sort of pressure Brazil

00:08:44.270 --> 00:08:46.049
into doing something to reign in

00:08:46.049 --> 00:08:47.740
the destruction of the Amazon,

00:08:47.740 --> 00:08:49.460
President Biden invited
President Bolsonaro

00:08:49.460 --> 00:08:52.470
to a climate summit that was held in 2021.

00:08:52.470 --> 00:08:53.610
President Bolsonaro.

00:08:53.610 --> 00:08:54.740
He was defiant.

00:08:54.740 --> 00:08:59.740
What he pointed to was this
idea of an Amazonian paradox.

00:09:00.270 --> 00:09:04.150
What he meant by that was
that Brazil's rainforest

00:09:04.150 --> 00:09:06.450
is one of the greatest
resources in the world,

00:09:07.330 --> 00:09:09.740
and yet 24 million people who live

00:09:09.740 --> 00:09:12.803
around the Amazon region, they're poor.

00:09:14.430 --> 00:09:16.650
So he basically said if the United States

00:09:16.650 --> 00:09:18.860
or any other richer nation in the world

00:09:18.860 --> 00:09:21.890
wants Brazil to stop deforestation,

00:09:21.890 --> 00:09:22.990
they gotta pay for it.

00:09:33.380 --> 00:09:35.490
Just getting to the Amazon is a hike.

00:09:35.490 --> 00:09:36.860
I live in San Paulo.

00:09:36.860 --> 00:09:39.113
Just flying there is
a really long journey.

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:43.060
There's no cell service,
the roads are terrible.

00:09:43.060 --> 00:09:45.963
And so anywhere you go is a journey.

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:50.590
We visit one town where this had happened

00:09:50.590 --> 00:09:54.260
approximately 20 years ago
called Uniao Bandeirantes.

00:09:54.260 --> 00:09:56.910
And this is a town that
really didn't exist

00:09:56.910 --> 00:09:58.780
except in the last 20 years.

00:09:58.780 --> 00:10:00.580
It was pure rain forest.

00:10:02.010 --> 00:10:03.840
And now it's almost 2,000 families

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:05.790
ended up settling there.

00:10:05.790 --> 00:10:08.470
Everywhere you look, you
see coffee plantations

00:10:08.470 --> 00:10:10.120
and cattle ranches,

00:10:10.120 --> 00:10:12.810
and you have thousands
and thousands of farmers

00:10:12.810 --> 00:10:14.860
who have been living on this land

00:10:14.860 --> 00:10:16.453
that they don't have title to.

00:10:18.050 --> 00:10:19.680
One of the most surprising aspects

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:21.440
of the journey that we took was that

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:24.160
it's really hard to find
clear villains there.

00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:25.420
I think from the outside,

00:10:25.420 --> 00:10:27.390
you look at the Amazon and you think,

00:10:27.390 --> 00:10:29.870
well, you know, monsters

00:10:29.870 --> 00:10:32.990
that are tearing down
this beautiful rainforest.

00:10:32.990 --> 00:10:35.500
When you get there and you
start talking to these people,

00:10:35.500 --> 00:10:37.780
you realize that some of
the decisions they've made

00:10:37.780 --> 00:10:39.740
are decisions that other
people would have made

00:10:39.740 --> 00:10:41.183
if they were in those shoes.

00:10:48.460 --> 00:10:50.940
One of the farmers that
we spent some time with

00:10:50.940 --> 00:10:52.040
is a man named Baiano.

00:10:53.050 --> 00:10:55.520
A few decades ago, he traded a motorcycle

00:10:55.520 --> 00:10:56.610
for a plot of land.

00:10:56.610 --> 00:11:01.463
And 20 years later, he
has built a mini empire.

00:11:03.380 --> 00:11:07.520
He was a small farmer
with 200 hectares or so,

00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:12.520
and he'd made the transition
from cattle to coffee.

00:11:12.920 --> 00:11:14.820
It was something he was very proud of.

00:11:17.530 --> 00:11:20.280
One of the arguments that you
hear from environmentalists

00:11:20.280 --> 00:11:21.430
and from people looking abroad

00:11:21.430 --> 00:11:23.200
is that there should be sustainable ways

00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:25.183
to live in harmony with the forest.

00:11:51.090 --> 00:11:53.460
There's no market for the
products that you make

00:11:53.460 --> 00:11:57.160
when you are producing from the forest.

00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:17.883
Another man who we met was
named Everaldo Pandolfi.

00:12:18.830 --> 00:12:20.890
He was one of the original settlers

00:12:20.890 --> 00:12:23.320
of Uniao Bandeirantes.

00:12:23.320 --> 00:12:28.300
He arrived in around 2000
with a few of his brothers,

00:12:28.300 --> 00:12:31.193
and it was pure rainforest
when they arrived.

00:12:32.550 --> 00:12:34.370
Pandolfi recognizes that

00:12:34.370 --> 00:12:37.350
to make a living on the land,
you have to clear the land.

00:12:37.350 --> 00:12:38.490
That's just the way it is.

00:12:38.490 --> 00:12:40.713
There's no other way to do it in his view.

00:12:41.860 --> 00:12:43.380
He went for the hardwoods first,

00:12:43.380 --> 00:12:45.790
because that brings in the fast cash.

00:12:45.790 --> 00:12:48.300
He torched the scrub to clean it up,

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:50.273
and then he put cattle on the land.

00:13:16.760 --> 00:13:17.593
That's what was repeated

00:13:17.593 --> 00:13:19.980
over and over again in
this particular town,

00:13:19.980 --> 00:13:23.060
and it's been repeated across the Amazon,

00:13:23.060 --> 00:13:25.570
and it's really a driving force behind

00:13:25.570 --> 00:13:27.633
the deforestation that we're seeing now.

00:13:34.490 --> 00:13:36.160
On the other end of this story,

00:13:36.160 --> 00:13:40.110
you have the industrialized
ranchers and farmers.

00:13:40.110 --> 00:13:43.510
Some of these guys have
amassed land empires

00:13:43.510 --> 00:13:45.730
in the tens of thousands of hectares,

00:13:45.730 --> 00:13:48.040
which is possible only if

00:13:48.040 --> 00:13:50.260
you were awarded it by the dictatorship,

00:13:50.260 --> 00:13:52.320
you bought it up from small farmers

00:13:52.320 --> 00:13:55.073
or they're land grabs themselves.

00:13:57.940 --> 00:14:01.422
One of the rancher we met,
a very successful rancher,

00:14:01.422 --> 00:14:03.060
Adelio Barofaldi.

00:14:03.060 --> 00:14:05.000
His history is like the
history of so many people

00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:06.470
that have settled in the Amazon.

00:14:06.470 --> 00:14:09.910
He migrated to the Amazon
from another part of Brazil

00:14:11.060 --> 00:14:13.803
in search of prosperity that
he couldn't find at home.

00:14:15.007 --> 00:14:18.970
Barofaldi is a staunch
supporter of Bolsonaro

00:14:18.970 --> 00:14:22.330
as are most of the big ranchers
and farmers in the state

00:14:22.330 --> 00:14:23.530
and throughout the Amazon.

00:14:23.530 --> 00:14:25.860
These are the guys that Bolsonaro says

00:14:25.860 --> 00:14:29.000
can solve the problem of deforestation.

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:33.250
And so as Bolsonaro is seeking to amnesty

00:14:33.250 --> 00:14:35.380
a lot of these land grabs,

00:14:35.380 --> 00:14:37.730
the biggest change that he's proposing

00:14:37.730 --> 00:14:40.620
is allowing these big farmers

00:14:40.620 --> 00:14:43.670
who have deforested more recently

00:14:43.670 --> 00:14:47.953
to apply for official
title to this Amazon land.

00:14:51.270 --> 00:14:53.190
Over the years, Adelio Barofaldi

00:14:53.190 --> 00:14:56.870
has amassed over 40,000 hectares of land,

00:14:56.870 --> 00:15:00.360
and he's strived to actually
follow the law in Brazil.

00:15:00.360 --> 00:15:03.000
He built his cattle ranching empire

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:06.370
on a system that is designed
to be gamed in a way.

00:15:06.370 --> 00:15:09.900
And he has actually tried, in his view,

00:15:09.900 --> 00:15:11.620
is to really follow the rules in Brazil

00:15:11.620 --> 00:15:14.513
that do allow you to go in
and develop a cattle ranch.

00:15:36.720 --> 00:15:39.840
He actually replanted
a lot of the preserve

00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:42.333
that he's required to
have under federal law.

00:15:45.120 --> 00:15:46.560
These are the guys that'll say

00:15:46.560 --> 00:15:50.330
that are the biggest advocates
for privatizing the Amazon,

00:15:50.330 --> 00:15:52.230
because they say they have the resources

00:15:52.230 --> 00:15:54.430
to protect the Amazon.

00:15:54.430 --> 00:15:56.900
And what you're seeing
now is the land invasion

00:15:56.900 --> 00:15:58.230
sort of flipping on its head

00:15:58.230 --> 00:16:01.970
in that you have large property owners

00:16:01.970 --> 00:16:04.690
who are still fighting for that land

00:16:04.690 --> 00:16:06.150
because they don't actually own title

00:16:06.150 --> 00:16:07.580
to a lot of this stuff.

00:16:07.580 --> 00:16:10.470
So they're still fighting for
this land by smaller farmers

00:16:10.470 --> 00:16:13.120
who are trying to break
into their reserves

00:16:13.120 --> 00:16:14.370
and cut down their trees.

00:16:21.640 --> 00:16:25.130
Over the years, the
public undesignated land

00:16:25.130 --> 00:16:26.720
has pretty much been snapped up.

00:16:26.720 --> 00:16:31.020
What's left in the Amazon
are protected parks

00:16:31.020 --> 00:16:33.200
and indigenous territories.

00:16:33.200 --> 00:16:36.253
And so those are the lands
that are now under attack.

00:16:37.220 --> 00:16:39.030
There are people that are trying to fight

00:16:39.030 --> 00:16:43.600
this sort of wave of
destruction as best they can.

00:16:43.600 --> 00:16:46.840
And one of them is a park ranger.

00:16:46.840 --> 00:16:49.110
We met a man named Carlos Rangel.

00:16:49.110 --> 00:16:51.030
He's a 72-year-old park ranger.

00:16:51.030 --> 00:16:52.100
He's ready to retire.

00:16:52.100 --> 00:16:54.190
He's been fighting this fight for 20 years

00:16:54.190 --> 00:16:57.660
and he will plain say he's exhausted.

00:16:57.660 --> 00:17:00.220
He dreams of retiring

00:17:00.220 --> 00:17:02.030
to write the great Brazilian novel,

00:17:02.030 --> 00:17:03.960
but he's afraid to leave his job

00:17:03.960 --> 00:17:06.280
because he's afraid that his replacement

00:17:06.280 --> 00:17:08.410
won't have the same sort of drive

00:17:08.410 --> 00:17:10.163
to protect the land.

00:17:11.250 --> 00:17:15.450
He oversees a park that is
twice the size of Rhode Island,

00:17:15.450 --> 00:17:19.363
and he does it with a
staff of just a few people.

00:17:20.610 --> 00:17:23.700
It's basically been a long
campaign to fight back,

00:17:23.700 --> 00:17:25.970
to keep back people who
want to go into his park

00:17:25.970 --> 00:17:28.350
and turn it into cattle ranches.

00:17:28.350 --> 00:17:31.210
He goes out into the
woods with his rangers

00:17:31.210 --> 00:17:33.150
and he's hacking his way through the woods

00:17:33.150 --> 00:17:34.323
with the machetes.

00:18:00.700 --> 00:18:02.650
It's incredibly difficult work,

00:18:02.650 --> 00:18:05.650
because the forest, you go 10 feet

00:18:05.650 --> 00:18:08.130
and you can't see the person anymore.

00:18:08.130 --> 00:18:10.053
It's easy for them to escape.

00:18:11.260 --> 00:18:16.223
Sometimes, it'll take 12
hours to get 20 kilometers.

00:18:17.200 --> 00:18:19.440
We weren't able to make those journeys

00:18:19.440 --> 00:18:22.300
because it was just so
deep into the forest

00:18:22.300 --> 00:18:23.550
that they were happening.

00:18:30.080 --> 00:18:35.080
Brazil does need to resolve
it's land titling system.

00:18:35.090 --> 00:18:37.240
It's a system that is so dysfunctional,

00:18:37.240 --> 00:18:41.283
that in many cases, you don't
know who the real owner is.

00:18:42.600 --> 00:18:44.250
A lot of people are making a lot of money

00:18:44.250 --> 00:18:47.240
from the dysfunctional
land management system

00:18:47.240 --> 00:18:49.160
that's in place.

00:18:49.160 --> 00:18:52.010
There's an incentive to keep that going,

00:18:52.010 --> 00:18:55.140
to not fix this broken system,

00:18:55.140 --> 00:18:58.190
because otherwise Brazil
would run out of space

00:18:58.190 --> 00:19:01.693
to feed the world's
appetite for commodities.

00:19:02.920 --> 00:19:07.800
It's a really complicated
and nuanced phenomenon.

00:19:07.800 --> 00:19:11.600
It's just not gonna be solved
unless you really recognize

00:19:11.600 --> 00:19:14.730
the social, economic,
political, and cultural forces

00:19:14.730 --> 00:19:17.950
that are behind that drive
destruction in the Amazon.

